  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on identifying 'probable' outcomes (those above 50% probability) and comparing the average of such outcomes. In this case, both lotteries have only the zero outcome as 'probable', leading to indifference. The individual’s reasoning also centers on the high likelihood of zero outcomes and treats the positive outcomes as negligible due to their low probabilities, which reflects a focus on the most likely (i.e., probable) result. Although the individual misstates the direction of the outcome difference (claiming A has higher gain), the core of the reasoning — dismissing the low-probability gains and focusing on the near-certainty of zero — aligns with the conceptual basis of the decision reason. The individual does not explicitly compute averages of probable outcomes, but the emphasis on the dominant zero outcomes and the consequent indifference to the rare gains matches the logic of the reason. Therefore, the reasoning process described is consistent with the application of the decision reason, even if not formalized.",
